Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A warm, period live-action fairy tale. In a stocking hangs the VELVETEEN RABBIT: fat and bunchy, spotted brown and white, with thread whiskers, pink sateen ears, black boot-button eyes, and a holly sprig between his paws.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

EDWARD, six, tousle-haired and bright-eyed in a blue wool suit, discovers the Rabbit among oranges, nuts, a toy engine, chocolate almonds, and a clockwork mouse.

For two hours Edward carries the Rabbit everywhere. Then the nursery fills with parcels and rustling tissue paper, and the Rabbit is left on the floor beneath the stocking.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

The polished toy engine clicks importantly. A chipped model boat displays its rigging. TIMOTHY, a jointed wooden lion, sits stiffly near the cupboard.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The mechanical toys were very superior, and pretended they were real.

The Rabbit lies near the fender beside the SKIN HORSE, an old brown toy with bald patches, exposed seams, and only a few hairs remaining in his tail.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time. That's why it doesn't happen often to people who break easily, or have sharp edges, or who have to be carefully kept.

SKIN HORSE
Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby.

SKIN HORSE
But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago; but once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

The Rabbit studies his bright whiskers and smooth velveteen, wishing for the magic but fearing its cost.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

EDWARD, now in striped sleeping clothes, searches his bed for his missing china dog. NANA, a brisk nursemaid in a charcoal dress and white cap, opens the toy cupboard.

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She lifts the Rabbit by one ear and drops him into Edward's arms.

Edward hugs the Rabbit beneath the counterpane. He makes tunnels in the bedclothes and whispers games while Nana's night-light burns on the mantel.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Very soon he grew to like it, for the Boy used to talk to him, and made nice tunnels for him under the bedclothes that he said were like the burrows the real rabbits lived in.

Night after night, Edward sleeps with both hands clasped around the Rabbit. The Rabbit's nose loses its pink, his tail begins to loosen, and his fine velveteen grows rubbed and soft.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Spring. Edward wheels the Rabbit through the garden in a small wheelbarrow, spreads a picnic cloth on the grass, and builds a fairy hut beneath the raspberry canes.

At dusk, Edward is called indoors and forgets the Rabbit in a flower-bed burrow. Dew darkens the Rabbit's velveteen.

Nana finds him with a candle, wet and earthy, and rubs him with the corner of her apron.

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

EDWARD sits upright in bed and reaches for him.

EDWARD
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

The Rabbit rests under Edward's chin, his dulled eyes shining with sudden understanding.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The nursery magic had happened to him. He was a toy no longer. He was Real; the Boy himself had said it.

EXT. WOOD BRACKEN - DAY

A long June evening. Edward makes the Rabbit a nest in the bracken before running away to pick flowers and play among the trees.

MOSS, a small chestnut-brown wild rabbit, and LARK, a grey-brown wild rabbit, creep from the tall ferns. Their bodies lengthen and bunch as they move.

MOSS
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

MOSS hops sideways and rises on his hind legs.

MOSS
Ho! It's as easy as anything. I don't believe you can!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I can! I can jump higher than anything!

MOSS
Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Rabbit sits very still. His back is one sewn piece, like a pincushion.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't want to.

MOSS cranes his neck, then laughs.

MOSS
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I have! I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

MOSS
Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

Moss whirls and dances over the grass. The Rabbit watches, longing to follow.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still!

Moss comes close enough for his whiskers to brush the Rabbit's ear. He wrinkles his nose, flattens his ears, and springs backward.

MOSS
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

Edward's footsteps crash through the bracken. Moss and Lark flash away, white tails vanishing between the ferns.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

Only ants run between his velvet paws as the bracken sways empty around him.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Late summer. Edward plays among the flower-beds with the Rabbit tucked under one arm.

The Rabbit has lost all his whiskers. The pink ear-lining has turned grey, his brown spots have faded, and he scarcely keeps his rabbit shape.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
To him he was always beautiful, and that was all that the little Rabbit cared about.

Edward kisses the Rabbit's worn nose and holds him close.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Edward lies feverish beneath the bedclothes, his face flushed and his small body burning hot. DR. HARRIS, an elderly doctor in a dark waistcoat, comes and goes while Nana tends the oil lamp.

The Rabbit stays hidden under the covers, pressed against Edward's side.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He never stirred, for he was afraid that if they found him some one might take him away, and he knew that the Boy needed him.

Days pass in the same lamplight. Edward murmurs in sleep; the Rabbit waits patiently beside the pillow.

The fever breaks. Edward sits up, pale but smiling, and looks at picture-books with the Rabbit cuddled against him.

EXT. BEDROOM BALCONY - MORNING

A bright morning. Edward, wrapped in a shawl, sits in fresh air among bedclothes. The Rabbit lies tangled beside him, his head peeping out.

A small valise waits by the open door. Edward watches sunlight on the garden, imagining the sea.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
“Hurray!” thought the little Rabbit. “To-morrow we shall go to the seaside!”

DR. HARRIS speaks quietly to Nana at the balcony door.

DR. HARRIS
The room must be disinfected. All the books and toys that he has played with in bed must be burnt.

Nana notices the Rabbit.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

DR. HARRIS
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once. What? Nonsense! Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

The Rabbit's hopeful face disappears beneath the bedclothes.

EXT. GARDEN BEHIND FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

HOBBS, the old gardener in muddy boots, leaves an untied sack behind the fowl-house. Old picture-books, scraps, and the Rabbit spill against its opening.

In an upstairs window, Edward sleeps in another bedroom. A new white plush bunny with glass eyes lies untouched beside him.

The Rabbit wriggles until his head reaches the mouth of the sack. He shivers in the cold, his worn coat too thin to protect him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He seemed to see them all pass before him: the fairy huts in the flower-bed, the quiet evenings in the wood when the little ants ran over his paws, the wonderful day when he first knew that he was Real.

The Rabbit looks toward the tall raspberry canes, black against the moonlit garden.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A real tear slides down his shabby velvet nose and falls into the earth.

Where it lands, slender emerald leaves rise from the ground. A golden-cupped flower opens between them.

The NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY steps from the blossom. Her gown is pearl and dew-drops; flowers circle her hair and throat.

She gathers the Rabbit in her arms and kisses his damp nose.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I have seen your face before, but I can't think where.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

The Fairy holds him close and rises into the moonlit wood.

EXT. WOOD GLADE - MOONLIT NIGHT

Silver bracken surrounds a velvet-green glade. Moss and Lark dance with their shadows until the Fairy descends among them.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

She sets the Rabbit on the grass and kisses him once more.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

The Rabbit remains still, ashamed to show his sewn-up back. Something tickles his nose. He lifts a hind toe to scratch it.

He freezes, staring at a living hind leg.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Instead of dingy velveteen he had brown fur, soft and shiny; his ears twitched by themselves, and his whiskers were so long that they brushed the grass.

The Rabbit leaps. Then he springs sideways, whirls in the grass, and races with Moss and Lark through the glade.

When he stops to look back, the Fairy has gone.

EXT. WOOD BRACKEN - DAY

Spring returns. Edward, healthy again and a little taller, plays among the bracken behind the house.

Moss, brown all over, creeps from the ferns. Beside him is a second wild rabbit with soft brown fur and faint pale markings beneath it, as though he had once been spotted.

Edward sees the marked rabbit and smiles with startled recognition.

EDWARD (V.O.)
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The Rabbit watches the child for one quiet moment, then turns to Moss and Lark.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But he never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

The three rabbits bound into the sunlit bracken.

> FADE OUT.
